On Fri, May 04, 2001 at 10:01:32AM +0100, cetus@snowball.ucd.ie wrote:
> What I ended up with was checking the actual IRQ number from SRM (it
> happened to be 10) and hardcoding it into usb-ohci.c, function
> ohci_pci_probe, at the very beginning, just before the check
>         if (!dev->irq)
> I just put dev->irq = 10; there and it worked.

IRQ number 10 means that it's routed via ISA interrupt controller.
BTW, on sx164 USB irq is also 10 according to the docs.
Probably it should be fixed by yet another Cypress PCI quirk...

Ivan.




